Return `effects().ignore()` for event types you don't handle. Only let genuine processing errors propagate, because an exception triggers redelivery. That's the contract for all Consumer sources.

```java
public Effect onEvent(CounterEvent event) {
  return switch (event) {
    case CounterEvent.ValueIncreased v -> { /* side effect */ yield effects().done(); }
    default                            -> effects().ignore();   // not our concern
  };
}
```

- For a sealed event type, list the cases you skip explicitly. That way the compiler flags any new event type you haven't decided on. Use `default` only if you want to skip all future types too.
- Delivery is at-least-once. Real failures will still redeliver, so make the handled paths idempotent.

